      "writtenOn": "2023-11-01T23:46:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for bringing this up, these pedantic size safety checks are something I wanted guidance on.\n\n`max_msgs_per_xfer` is `unsigned short` because `module_param` doesn\u0027t support fixed sizes.  While `short` is 16 bits on any Linux system I\u0027ve programmed for, AFAIK its absolute size is not guaranteed by C.  In theory it could be 32 bits, for example.\n\nDoes Linux guarantee short is \u003c\u003d 16 bits?  Is there a reference for Linux type size guarantees beyond what C requires?\n\nOr is this something where kernel style is to just code for the architectures that exist today and avoid complexity for (nonsensical?) hypotheticals like 32 bit `short`? :-)",
      "parentUuid": "8992f4ed_1153aeb5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 156,
        "startChar": 29,
        "endLine": 156,
        "endChar": 46
      },
